From 1e8c39aaf8e29135c1af46175d3e53e07bc7fec3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 02:02:55 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] gallium/pvr: 512 end-of-tile cache entries - mip-chain scenes
 were recompiling their EOT programs

The end-of-tile program cache held 32 (target set -> compiled USC program) entries. A scene that
renders many distinct target sets per frame - terrain renders about 27 (mip levels and bloom passes,
each a new address+format set) - overflowed it and fell to an eviction pattern that missed 83% of the
time; a miss costs full pco compilation plus a USC and a PDS upload, measured at ~200 us per render,
i.e. several milliseconds of CPU per frame.

Growing the cache to 512 entries (~50 KB) holds every target set of such a scene: terrain's cache hits
go from 1464/9415 to 9802/9885 in the steady state, its per-flush EOT cost from ~200 us to ~1 us, and
its per-frame user CPU from 12.1 ms to 1.9 ms (system 3.2 ms). Terrain itself only moves from 18 to
19-20 fps because it is GPU-bound, so the full glmark2 score is unchanged (809) - this is a CPU
overhead fix that removes a per-render compile from multi-target workloads, not a scene win.

Verified: full gltri gate (49 modes, all native glGetError 0x0), rttest PASS, rt8test, MRT/sRGB/
instancing/extension tests unchanged, 0 MMU faults; terrain measured with --results fps:cpu before
(12.1 ms user) and after (1.9 ms user) the change.
---
 src/gallium/drivers/pvr_native/pvr_draw.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/pvr_native/pvr_draw.c b/src/gallium/drivers/pvr_native/pvr_draw.c
index 43555d28..937de365 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/pvr_native/pvr_draw.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/pvr_native/pvr_draw.c
@@ -160,7 +160,13 @@ struct pvr_draw_state {
    float zclear;
 
    /* Caches. */
-   struct pvr_draw_eot eot[32];
+   /* End-of-tile programs, per target set (addresses and formats). Each entry is a compiled USC
+    * program: a miss costs pco compilation plus a USC and a PDS upload, which was 200 us per render
+    * on the terrain scene. A mip-chain-heavy scene renders dozens of distinct level sizes per frame
+    * (terrain: ~27 target sets, 83% miss rate with 32 entries); 512 entries hold every set of such a
+    * scene for ~50 KB and turns the steady state into cache hits. Entries are never freed: the
+    * programs stay allocated on the PDS heap either way. */
+   struct pvr_draw_eot eot[512];
    unsigned nr_eot;
    struct pvr_draw_bg bg[4];
    struct pvr_draw_load load[8];
-- 
2.47.3

